<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Westphalia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Westphalia is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, centred on Münster and reaching south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>. It was originally the western part of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>; after 1180 the title of Duke of Westphalia passed to the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, while most of the region was divided among bishoprics and counties. It became a Prussian province in 1815 and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westphalia.jpg" alt="Westphalia" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rüdiger Wölk · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuensterPrinzipalmarkt09.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Westphalia covers about 20,210 square kilometres and has some 7.9 million inhabitants. Its north belongs to the North German Plain and is mostly flat, while the German Central Uplands rise in the south. The region lies roughly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, both north and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, and the Ems and the Lippe are other important rivers. Its highest summits are the Langenberg, at 843 metres, and the Kahler Asten, at 842 metres, both in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauerland">Sauerland</a> part of the Rothaar Mountains. Its counterpart to the east is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 1 CE there were numerous incursions through the region and perhaps some Roman or Romanized settlements. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> took place near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, then a settlement of the Westphalians, who were part of the Germanic tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Charlemagne is thought to have spent considerable time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> were partly fought here. Westphalia was originally a district of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Angria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordalbingia">Nordalbingia</a>. After the deposition of the Saxon duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> in 1180, Emperor Barbarossa made it a duchy in its own right, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> covered only a small area south of the Lippe.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> left no dominant religion: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> was strong in the east and north, while Münster and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> were Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> was divided almost equally. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>, signed in Münster and Osnabrück in 1648, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and the idea of state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> that came from it became known as Westphalian sovereignty. After the Treaty of Tilsit in 1807 the easternmost part of the region belonged to the French client Kingdom of Westphalia until 1813, a state that took in mostly Hessian and Eastphalian lands. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a> in 1815. The former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> and the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a> joined it in 1816 and the condominium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippstadt">Lippstadt</a> in 1851.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1946 the British military government joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Prussian Province of Westphalia</a> with the northern half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, and the former Free State of Lippe joined in 1947. No administrative division bears the name Westphalia now, but its inhabitants call themselves Westphalians. The seventeen districts and nine independent cities of the region, with the district of Lippe, belong to the Westphalia-Lippe Regional Association. The region's emblem is the Westphalian Steed, a white horse on a red field derived from the Saxon Steed of the medieval Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. The Westphalian variant of Low German is spoken in the region and beyond its borders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Westphalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers from Saxony, <b>Westphalia</b> and Flanders moved east of the Elbe into former Slav lands, changing the population, farming and economy of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Bremen's wealth came from the Weser, which linked the city to the North Sea and to a hinterland reaching into <b>Westphalia</b> and Lower Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna redrew his borders: in 1816 he ceded <b>Westphalia</b> to Prussia and Hesse-Homburg was restored as a separate landgraviate, while he received Rhenish Hesse on the left bank of the Rhine, with Mainz, Worms, Bingen and Alzey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Westphalia</b> is often credited with articulating the principle of territorial sovereignty that shaped the modern international state system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the Rhineland and <b>Westphalia</b>, gained in 1815, made Prussia an industrial power, with coal and iron in the Ruhr and Upper Silesia, firms such as Krupp, a railway network from the 1840s and the Zollverein customs union, launched under Prussian leadership in 1834, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>German merchants, many from Lübeck and <b>Westphalia</b>, settled below the castle, and Dominican friars founded the convent of St Catherine in the 1240s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
